WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:09.100
I'm happy to welcome Markus Rabe
from UC Berkeley here today to

00:00:09.101 --> 00:00:11.829
give us a talk about
program synthesis, and

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:16.190
quantify elimination, and
QBF, and things like that.

00:00:16.191 --> 00:00:19.770
So Markus has been there for
a couple of years, and

00:00:19.771 --> 00:00:25.830
this is a Physicist in Saarland
University with on Hyper LTL.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.300
So, I'm happy to have you here,
and so,

00:00:29.301 --> 00:00:31.724
not to take away more time,
please.

00:00:32.885 --> 00:00:35.555
&gt;&gt; Thanks for the introduction.

00:00:35.556 --> 00:00:39.665
Yeah, my background is more in
informational security, for

00:00:39.666 --> 00:00:41.265
methods temporal logics,
and so on.

00:00:41.266 --> 00:00:45.005
But recently I've been more
shifting over to the synthesis

00:00:45.006 --> 00:00:48.045
aspect of formal methods,
mostly, and

00:00:48.046 --> 00:00:52.810
my overall goal is to apply
this back into security,

00:00:52.811 --> 00:00:54.930
where I think it would
have a tremendous use.

00:00:54.931 --> 00:00:57.410
So while earlier,
or previously, or

00:00:57.411 --> 00:01:01.750
some years back we still thought
that critical programming errors

00:01:01.751 --> 00:01:04.790
result in the some things
like that, or this.

00:01:04.791 --> 00:01:09.280
Today it looks more like that,
and these are like scams

00:01:09.281 --> 00:01:11.360
that lock your computer and
you can only unlock them for

00:01:11.361 --> 00:01:14.390
money, or like that where
someone hacks into your car and

00:01:14.391 --> 00:01:16.910
you end up crashing your car.

00:01:16.911 --> 00:01:22.890
So, I think, there is a shift in

00:01:22.891 --> 00:01:29.580
the attention from the safety
aspects to the security aspects,

00:01:29.581 --> 00:01:33.990
and that is, I guess, mostly
driven by the economic impact,

00:01:33.991 --> 00:01:36.240
which is estimated in
the many billions.

00:01:36.241 --> 00:01:39.740
These are obviously people
who are profiting from

00:01:39.741 --> 00:01:42.620
a high estimate here,
but I think it's

00:01:42.621 --> 00:01:46.860
fair to say there is a really
big economic push towards it.

00:01:46.861 --> 00:01:49.230
I think today might even
be the tipping point for

00:01:49.231 --> 00:01:52.162
like people taking security
more serious, because today,

00:01:52.163 --> 00:01:55.700
I think, it is the first CEO
who had to step down because of

00:01:55.701 --> 00:01:57.270
a big data breach.

00:01:57.271 --> 00:01:58.950
So that may actually
change something.

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:00.840
&gt;&gt; He's not the first one.
&gt;&gt; And he's not the last one.

00:02:00.841 --> 00:02:01.900
&gt;&gt; He's not the first one,
really?

00:02:01.901 --> 00:02:02.960
&gt;&gt; I don't think so.

00:02:03.978 --> 00:02:06.695
At least with a major company,
as far as I recall,

00:02:06.696 --> 00:02:07.935
maybe there are more.

00:02:07.936 --> 00:02:11.450
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think he's the number

00:02:11.451 --> 00:02:13.427
one reason why?

00:02:13.428 --> 00:02:15.012
I've heard that quote.

00:02:15.013 --> 00:02:18.447
&gt;&gt; Okay, [LAUGH] I mean they're
definitely concerned about

00:02:18.448 --> 00:02:20.280
it now, yeah [LAUGH].

00:02:20.281 --> 00:02:24.523
&gt;&gt; Point, maybe the tipping
point is happening even more.

00:02:24.524 --> 00:02:26.554
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Unless we hear whether this

00:02:26.555 --> 00:02:27.731
Springfield stuff is right.

00:02:27.732 --> 00:02:29.058
I've heard this
quote several times.

00:02:29.059 --> 00:02:30.968
&gt;&gt; Okay, okay, cool.

00:02:30.969 --> 00:02:32.566
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:33.671
&gt;&gt; So all the while,

00:02:33.672 --> 00:02:36.632
we actually know how to
design secure systems.

00:02:36.633 --> 00:02:41.336
There have been several major
attempts at formally verifying

00:02:41.337 --> 00:02:45.049
systems with a very high
standard of certainty.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:50.616
The seL4 Microkernel was maybe
the first real big example

00:02:50.617 --> 00:02:56.422
showing it, but there are major
other efforts in this area.

00:02:56.423 --> 00:03:02.158
However, they still
didn't reach practical,

00:03:02.159 --> 00:03:04.660
or the normal
software development,

00:03:04.661 --> 00:03:07.990
because it's still
pretty expensive, and

00:03:07.991 --> 00:03:12.710
I think that for
the cost reason, and for others,

00:03:12.711 --> 00:03:16.050
I think that program synthesis
will have a major impact,

00:03:16.051 --> 00:03:19.900
and I think that will be mostly
in three different ways.

00:03:19.901 --> 00:03:23.615
First of all, while the standard
view of program synthesis is

00:03:23.616 --> 00:03:26.188
that we can synthesize
performed code, or

00:03:26.189 --> 00:03:29.405
low-level code,
from high levels of abstraction,

00:03:29.406 --> 00:03:32.988
and that would reduce the
complexity in software designs.

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:36.438
It could help too, and
in particularly it could reduce

00:03:36.439 --> 00:03:40.564
the amount of low-level coding
that's really error prone.

00:03:40.565 --> 00:03:42.846
Now that is the sort of standard
view of problem synthesis, but

00:03:42.847 --> 00:03:45.042
I think there are different ways
how problem synthesis could

00:03:45.043 --> 00:03:45.660
be really used.

00:03:45.661 --> 00:03:49.480
It could all synthesize, and
prove objects for the improvers,

00:03:49.481 --> 00:03:54.110
which is I think would help to
directly lower the cost of these

00:03:54.111 --> 00:03:59.070
major verification efforts,
and we could also use that

00:03:59.071 --> 00:04:03.802
in an automated program analysis
in all it's variants, and

00:04:03.803 --> 00:04:05.360
synthesize things like
invariance, ranking functions,

00:04:05.361 --> 00:04:06.660
and so on.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.810
And by that at least
find in existing code,

00:04:10.811 --> 00:04:13.265
which is also very
helpful too of course.

00:04:13.266 --> 00:04:17.479
Now I see that, I considered
the problem synthesis isn't

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.630
a very general fashion here.

00:04:19.631 --> 00:04:23.700
I think that it typically has
this pattern where we ask well,

00:04:23.701 --> 00:04:27.050
does it exist some program or
some syntactic object or

00:04:27.051 --> 00:04:28.750
some class of things
that we can describe.

00:04:28.751 --> 00:04:31.570
There's that for all inputs.

00:04:31.571 --> 00:04:33.860
Some specification halt.

00:04:33.861 --> 00:04:37.351
Typically we can compile these
synthesis problems into such

00:04:37.352 --> 00:04:41.047
a format, and that could be also
existed proof, such that, for

00:04:41.048 --> 00:04:43.717
all possible sums of
the variables it is true,

00:04:43.718 --> 00:04:46.250
something like that
would kind of represent

00:04:46.251 --> 00:04:48.660
the pure improving aspect of it,
maybe.

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:54.790
But, I think, if we manage
to solve this problems,

00:04:54.791 --> 00:04:59.190
in a fairly general and fast
way, this could help in many

00:04:59.191 --> 00:05:04.300
different ways, and would really
help for software security.

00:05:04.301 --> 00:05:09.510
So, however, of course, I think
everyone is aware of that,

00:05:09.511 --> 00:05:12.080
program synthesis is really
not scalable so far.

00:05:12.081 --> 00:05:13.795
If we are really lucky,

00:05:13.796 --> 00:05:16.510
it scales to five to ten lines
of code, and, but for that we've

00:05:16.511 --> 00:05:18.620
probably already have to
restrict the program very much.

00:05:18.621 --> 00:05:23.460
So this is really the biggest
issue of that approach yet.

00:05:23.461 --> 00:05:27.430
So the approach that I want
to take is to identify

00:05:27.431 --> 00:05:32.299
the fundamental scalability
issues behind this, and for

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:36.891
that I want to reduce the
problem to its simplest form.

00:05:36.892 --> 00:05:40.403
To a very pure form where we can
actually analyze the problems,

00:05:40.404 --> 00:05:41.661
and that is I believe,

00:05:41.662 --> 00:05:44.994
it's synthesis problem is
quantified boolean formulas.

00:05:44.995 --> 00:05:48.100
We will even further restrict
it to just quantified boolean

00:05:48.101 --> 00:05:50.270
formula with one quantified
destination just to look

00:05:50.271 --> 00:05:53.820
at the core of the problem, and
that we'll study today, and for

00:05:53.821 --> 00:05:57.470
that I will just suggest
a new algorithm, and

00:05:57.471 --> 00:06:02.340
then we'll evaluate it, and for
that we started out with some

00:06:02.341 --> 00:06:05.210
synthesis benchmarks that
we're essentially, well,

00:06:05.211 --> 00:06:06.830
synthesizing some
proof objects and

00:06:06.831 --> 00:06:10.180
then we also took some
benchmarks from the sketch.

00:06:10.181 --> 00:06:12.170
Benchmarks really like
that is Amanda's.

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:16.480
Amanda helped us in generating,
basically,

00:06:16.481 --> 00:06:22.290
Boolean encodings off this
problem synthesis benchmarks.

00:06:22.291 --> 00:06:23.910
While a reason why
we picked that is,

00:06:23.911 --> 00:06:26.230
well obviously these
were available.

00:06:26.231 --> 00:06:28.540
We didn't have to
do it ourselves,

00:06:28.541 --> 00:06:31.620
which is I think a good
thing for benchmarking,

00:06:31.621 --> 00:06:34.087
because that means
you cannot cheat.

00:06:34.088 --> 00:06:37.500
We have the files first before
we developed the algorithm.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:42.170
Then all these three families
included some unsolved problems,

00:06:42.171 --> 00:06:46.280
and so it was possible to
measure some progress towards

00:06:46.281 --> 00:06:48.740
synthesis there, because I
don't believe that we can

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:50.960
suddenly scale to hundreds
of lines of code, so

00:06:50.961 --> 00:06:55.220
we didn't wanna set a goal
that was too large.

00:06:55.221 --> 00:06:56.780
Now just to be a bit
more concrete,

00:06:56.781 --> 00:07:00.280
the ranking function problem
has problems like that.

00:07:00.281 --> 00:07:01.380
We are given
the boolean problem,

00:07:01.381 --> 00:07:03.200
over some say variables, and

00:07:03.201 --> 00:07:06.863
we basically just ask whether
it's just a constant C,

00:07:06.864 --> 00:07:11.403
such that for all states x and x
prime if they are successes, so

00:07:11.404 --> 00:07:18.890
if x prime is the successor of
x, then the multiplication of

00:07:18.891 --> 00:07:23.952
the constant with a must
be smaller than the, well,

00:07:23.953 --> 00:07:29.430
the product of the content
with the previous date.

00:07:29.431 --> 00:07:32.140
So, a very simple encoding.

00:07:32.141 --> 00:07:36.490
That was done by Cook and
some others,

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:41.370
and they included programs
mostly taken from Windows device

00:07:41.371 --> 00:07:43.530
drivers, of course they
are reduced ones, but

00:07:43.531 --> 00:07:45.285
they had to be realistic bits.

00:07:45.286 --> 00:07:50.263
So they're encodings of 16 bit,
32 bit and 64 bit, and

00:07:50.264 --> 00:07:53.252
they have small
snippets like that.

00:07:53.253 --> 00:07:55.600
I think this is one of
the smallest snippets.

00:07:55.601 --> 00:07:58.286
It basically asks whether,
will it be given two variables.

00:07:58.287 --> 00:08:02.889
We only know about them that
they are equal on their last

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:07.193
five bits, apparently,
for whatever reason, or

00:08:07.194 --> 00:08:11.696
they're not equal on the last
five bits, sorry, and

00:08:11.697 --> 00:08:16.100
then we are counting one up,
and sorry, apparently

00:08:16.101 --> 00:08:21.003
someone had a small counter in
the last five bits of code and

00:08:21.004 --> 00:08:23.747
tried to have a loop over these.

00:08:23.748 --> 00:08:26.950
Okay, it turns out this is
a non-terminating program.

00:08:26.951 --> 00:08:31.830
Because if index is
different to have on the,

00:08:34.678 --> 00:08:38.855
Bits that it doesn't compare
it with, then we store that in

00:08:38.856 --> 00:08:43.379
the integer i and here in this
comparison, it will always fail.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:47.686
Because we can always count
up the last five bits but

00:08:47.687 --> 00:08:50.000
it will never do anything.

00:08:50.001 --> 00:08:55.356
So and as a human, you can spot
that and it's not that hard,

00:08:55.357 --> 00:09:00.848
but it's not so easy if you just
see a sea of classes and bits.

00:09:00.849 --> 00:09:03.421
And this is a problem that can
be encoded apparently in some

00:09:03.422 --> 00:09:05.426
3000 classes with
a 32 bit semantics.

00:09:05.427 --> 00:09:07.791
It has some 128 variable, so

00:09:07.792 --> 00:09:10.512
don't you wanna
enumerate things.

00:09:10.513 --> 00:09:15.314
And in the end, it turns out
we can solve these things, but

00:09:15.315 --> 00:09:19.441
very quickly within,
say 100 milliseconds.

00:09:19.442 --> 00:09:21.651
Okay, all right.

00:09:21.652 --> 00:09:24.188
&gt;&gt; So, [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure.

00:09:24.189 --> 00:09:28.540
&gt;&gt; I think most non-ranking
function exists for opposite.

00:09:28.541 --> 00:09:29.752
&gt;&gt; So they exist,
non ranking, yeah.

00:09:29.753 --> 00:09:32.192
And to prove the absence
is nontrivial,

00:09:32.193 --> 00:09:35.277
it is of course not
a particularly hard for humans,

00:09:35.278 --> 00:09:38.505
but we want to automize these
things and this kind of,

00:09:38.506 --> 00:09:42.998
I just want to justify that this
is a reasonable benchmark for synthesis-

00:09:42.999 --> 00:09:45.053
&gt;&gt; Both non-ranking function

00:09:45.054 --> 00:09:48.020
exists means it
does not terminate?

00:09:48.021 --> 00:09:48.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:09:48.581 --> 00:09:50.095
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; Yeah, exactly and

00:09:50.096 --> 00:09:54.978
the other problems we considered
were then, things like function

00:09:54.979 --> 00:09:59.000
summaries of Boolean programs
and program sketches.

00:09:59.001 --> 00:10:01.509
And these were sometimes
substantially larger, so

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:04.303
the synthesis problems we
considered were typically like

00:10:04.304 --> 00:10:08.080
50,000 clauses, some at 100,000,
200,000 clauses.

00:10:08.081 --> 00:10:11.307
But, I mean, this is not
particularly impressive for

00:10:11.308 --> 00:10:13.410
SMT solver or for
SaaS solvers, but

00:10:13.411 --> 00:10:17.267
we are trying to solve a harder
problem here, so that's kind of,

00:10:17.268 --> 00:10:20.447
I think, it's a reasonable
synthesis benchmarks.

00:10:20.448 --> 00:10:23.257
If we can do that,
we did some progress.

00:10:23.258 --> 00:10:31.848
Okay, this talk, I'll basically
go through this schedule here.

00:10:31.849 --> 00:10:34.918
And first of all, take a look
at the existing synthesis

00:10:34.919 --> 00:10:36.922
algorithms on
the program level and

00:10:36.923 --> 00:10:39.580
try to identify what
their problems are.

00:10:39.581 --> 00:10:40.820
And at least one problem,

00:10:40.821 --> 00:10:44.810
I mean, I'm not suggesting
that I'm solving everything.

00:10:44.811 --> 00:10:49.500
And then we'll do the step
to QBFN codings and

00:10:49.501 --> 00:10:52.335
we'll take a look at that and
see what the problems are with

00:10:52.336 --> 00:10:57.300
QBFN algorithms are and then
we'll go into the algorithm.

00:10:57.301 --> 00:10:59.874
We'll go through
the emulation and

00:10:59.875 --> 00:11:03.829
then we have two parts that
are kind of more an outlook.

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:06.465
There's one theoretical
work on how to lift this to

00:11:06.466 --> 00:11:08.062
reasoning about functions and

00:11:08.063 --> 00:11:10.345
some quantitative works
that also we need.

00:11:10.346 --> 00:11:14.578
I hope, we actually make it
through the last part, as well,

00:11:14.579 --> 00:11:16.824
will depend a bit on the timing.

00:11:16.825 --> 00:11:21.483
All right, CEGIS, this is a very
general algorithmic scheme to

00:11:21.484 --> 00:11:23.477
solve synthesis queries.

00:11:23.478 --> 00:11:28.094
And basically, we want to just
have a version for-looper,

00:11:28.095 --> 00:11:32.711
pick a candidate program,
wanna check whether it's the one

00:11:32.712 --> 00:11:37.327
we're looking for and if not,
then we exclude some programs

00:11:37.328 --> 00:11:40.066
from the next query,
and iterate.

00:11:40.067 --> 00:11:43.775
Well, we can see that as
a learning framework,

00:11:43.776 --> 00:11:47.579
people have been exploring
the theory on that and

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:51.217
anyways, there are two
main flavors of that.

00:11:51.218 --> 00:11:53.990
Which is the enumerative
synthesis approach and

00:11:53.991 --> 00:11:57.844
the SNT solver approach, maybe,
there's also this ecclesiastic

00:11:57.845 --> 00:12:00.670
one that I won't go
into too much today.

00:12:00.671 --> 00:12:02.770
And they both come
with their problems,

00:12:02.771 --> 00:12:05.780
like, the enumerative
approach is to enumerate

00:12:05.781 --> 00:12:08.040
syntactic expressions
from small to large.

00:12:08.041 --> 00:12:11.445
And kind of to always evaluate
them against the count examples

00:12:11.446 --> 00:12:12.697
you've found so far.

00:12:12.698 --> 00:12:16.151
And that is we can enumerate
things really quickly, but

00:12:16.152 --> 00:12:20.195
of course, it's clear that once
the programs we're looking for

00:12:20.196 --> 00:12:24.165
get too large, then a number of
possibilities just explodes and

00:12:24.166 --> 00:12:25.770
we won't get anywhere.

00:12:25.771 --> 00:12:32.720
Unless we restrict the grammar
so much that the number of

00:12:32.721 --> 00:12:35.800
possibilities is kind of small,
but then we have to sort-of know

00:12:35.801 --> 00:12:38.290
what we're looking for so it's
kind of not really a solution

00:12:38.291 --> 00:12:39.510
in the long run, I believe.

00:12:39.511 --> 00:12:43.228
So the other problem is to use
an SNT solver which does not

00:12:43.229 --> 00:12:46.947
necessarily suffer so
much from this explosion, but

00:12:46.948 --> 00:12:49.250
it turns out,
in practice, it does.

00:12:49.251 --> 00:12:53.070
Like after we add some
subqueries, SNT solvers tend to

00:12:53.071 --> 00:12:56.817
have real problems, at least,
on the even codings.

00:12:56.818 --> 00:12:59.874
I'm not sure whether they
are much better ones, but so

00:12:59.875 --> 00:13:03.329
far in literature, it seems that
often the problem is with SNT

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:06.386
solver was after adding
a number of counterexamples,

00:13:06.387 --> 00:13:08.880
things really start to get slow.

00:13:08.881 --> 00:13:12.320
And there's one problem that
both of these approaches share,

00:13:12.321 --> 00:13:12.850
which is that, for

00:13:12.851 --> 00:13:15.780
some examples, you just need
too many count examples.

00:13:15.781 --> 00:13:19.336
In particular, for
those where we have to

00:13:19.337 --> 00:13:24.120
refute the existence of
an object in a certain grammar.

00:13:24.121 --> 00:13:27.132
Let's take a look at this
very simple program sketch,

00:13:27.133 --> 00:13:30.405
I sort of think many people have
seen that [INAUDIBLE] has like

00:13:30.406 --> 00:13:32.314
promoted that kind
of thing a lot.

00:13:32.315 --> 00:13:36.540
This is a program snippet or
small function that just has one

00:13:36.541 --> 00:13:40.598
hole in it which is denoted by
these two question marks and

00:13:40.599 --> 00:13:44.232
the problem is to find
a constant by which you want to

00:13:44.233 --> 00:13:46.769
replace these two
question marks,

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:50.767
such that the program doesn’t
violate the desertion.

00:13:52.742 --> 00:13:56.440
Now the dysfunction is,
we supposed to do

00:13:56.441 --> 00:14:00.990
something really stupid, really
simple, it's supposed to return

00:14:00.991 --> 00:14:03.406
a number that is different
to the input, right?

00:14:03.407 --> 00:14:07.341
So we're given some y as
an input and we have to then

00:14:07.342 --> 00:14:12.232
the constant, but that is a
constant, it cannot depend on y.

00:14:12.233 --> 00:14:14.203
And then they are supposed
to be different,

00:14:14.204 --> 00:14:16.663
it is clear that such
