00:59:13.804 --> 00:59:17.622
We use a simple proof system for
DQBF that [INAUDIBLE] and

00:59:17.623 --> 00:59:22.480
now we're kind of short on time,
so I'm gonna make this fast.

00:59:22.481 --> 00:59:24.928
For that,
to talk about this briefly,

00:59:24.929 --> 00:59:27.598
we have to introduce
the actual notation of

00:59:27.599 --> 00:59:30.802
DQBF which doesn't usually
come with functions.

00:59:30.803 --> 00:59:34.959
Typically, it's formulated in
terms of quantifiers that there

00:59:34.960 --> 00:59:37.379
exists a function f
that has these inputs.

00:59:38.670 --> 00:59:42.618
Where these are all these
universal variables, and then we

00:59:42.619 --> 00:59:46.347
can use that f, which actually
is sort of the function of f2,

00:59:46.348 --> 00:59:48.673
these elements
inside that formula.

00:59:48.674 --> 00:59:52.340
Now, this is a sort of just
normal function where every

00:59:52.341 --> 00:59:54.823
function just has
one set of inputs.

00:59:54.824 --> 00:59:58.321
And actually it turns out, we
reproved [INAUDIBLE] we couldn't

00:59:58.322 --> 01:00:00.635
just revive that with
a linear blow-up.

01:00:00.636 --> 01:00:03.880
So we couldn't rewrite this
quantification of a function

01:00:03.881 --> 01:00:07.855
with a linear blow-up into that
quantifier representation,

01:00:07.856 --> 01:00:08.660
pretty easy.

01:00:08.661 --> 01:00:12.400
So we now just have formulas
like that, we say okay,

01:00:12.401 --> 01:00:16.490
we have some symbol y1,
that depends on the inputs x1.

01:00:16.491 --> 01:00:20.074
We have some symbol x2
that depends on x2, and

01:00:20.075 --> 01:00:23.580
let's just zoom into one clause,
x1, x2.

01:00:23.581 --> 01:00:26.247
The question that is really
important to ask here, or

01:00:26.248 --> 01:00:28.499
that really helped me
to understand this is,

01:00:28.500 --> 01:00:31.030
who's responsible for
satisfying that clause?

01:00:31.031 --> 01:00:33.425
If we're in SAT, that is,
if x1 and x2 are empty, so

01:00:33.426 --> 01:00:35.306
they're both
propositional variables,

01:00:35.307 --> 01:00:37.255
one of them statically
satisfies a clause.

01:00:37.256 --> 01:00:41.347
It has to for any solution here,
otherwise it would be violated.

01:00:41.348 --> 01:00:48.517
For QBF, we know that either x1
includes x2, or x2 includes x1.

01:00:48.518 --> 01:00:53.046
Probably because x2 occurs
behind x1 in the quantifier

01:00:53.047 --> 01:00:57.761
prefix, it includes maybe
more universal variables, and

01:00:57.762 --> 01:00:59.471
not at the other end.

01:00:59.472 --> 01:01:05.299
And then we can say, whenever
we cross multiply by y1,

01:01:05.300 --> 01:01:07.660
y2 has to satisfy it.

01:01:07.661 --> 01:01:12.652
And it can know because it
knows more universal variables.

01:01:12.653 --> 01:01:17.052
Anyways, now we can, in DQBF,
we don't have that property.

01:01:17.053 --> 01:01:22.204
This could be incomparable,
x1 and x2 could be just like,

01:01:22.205 --> 01:01:27.190
neither one includes [INAUDIBLE]
nor the other way around.

01:01:27.191 --> 01:01:32.612
Okay, in this case, and the
basic insight was that y1 and y2

01:01:32.613 --> 01:01:38.049
would have to coordinate based
on their shared information.

01:01:38.050 --> 01:01:43.400
So based only on the values
that occur both in x1 and x2,

01:01:43.401 --> 01:01:48.210
it has to be clear which
side satisfies the clause.

01:01:49.370 --> 01:01:54.481
And this gives rise
to our proof rule,

01:01:54.482 --> 01:02:03.115
which essentially says given a
clause with two separate parts.

01:02:03.116 --> 01:02:06.247
Where the one part cannot depend
on the dependencies of the other

01:02:06.248 --> 01:02:07.987
part, and
the other way around, so

01:02:07.988 --> 01:02:09.963
they're incomparable
dependencies.

01:02:09.964 --> 01:02:13.272
And then we can introduce
a fresh variable, t,

01:02:13.273 --> 01:02:17.341
that just depends on the section
of the two dependencies.

01:02:17.342 --> 01:02:21.065
And this variable t controls
which side of the clause has to

01:02:21.066 --> 01:02:23.042
satisfy the original clause.

01:02:23.043 --> 01:02:25.467
So we introduce a new function
that resolves this thing.

01:02:25.468 --> 01:02:28.709
We call these clauses
information forks,

01:02:28.710 --> 01:02:31.411
sort of,
there's a similar term in

01:02:31.412 --> 01:02:35.105
[INAUDIBLE] synthesis of
distributive systems,

01:02:35.106 --> 01:02:38.449
because it's sort of
the same effect here.

01:02:38.450 --> 01:02:40.610
Then we just need the two
standard rules for

01:02:40.611 --> 01:02:43.804
proving QBF formulas, resolution
and universal reduction.

01:02:43.805 --> 01:02:46.704
Universal reduction just says,
if we have a class full

01:02:46.705 --> 01:02:50.120
of universal, we can drop them
all, create the anti-clause.

01:02:50.121 --> 01:02:53.468
Now, in the example
that comes with this,

01:02:53.469 --> 01:02:57.751
this is just the previous
example with our functions.

01:02:57.752 --> 01:03:00.644
Just that now the functions
are called, I mean, with this

01:03:00.645 --> 01:03:03.672
equality of the functions,
if and only if x1 and x2 holds.

01:03:03.673 --> 01:03:08.051
Just that now the f1 and f2
are unfortunately called y1, y2.

01:03:08.052 --> 01:03:11.848
So what we just do is we split
all the clauses, because they

01:03:11.849 --> 01:03:15.112
all have this property
that they have y1 and y2.

01:03:15.113 --> 01:03:18.084
And we introduce these
variables t1 to t6, and

01:03:18.085 --> 01:03:20.920
choose positively here and
negatively here.

01:03:20.921 --> 01:03:25.406
They all have anti-dependencies
because the intersection of set

01:03:25.407 --> 01:03:27.093
of x1 and x2 are empty.

01:03:27.094 --> 01:03:29.382
And then we just
apply resolution,

01:03:29.383 --> 01:03:32.138
we get a formula only
over the variables t.

01:03:32.139 --> 01:03:35.437
We apply more resolution in our
anti-clause, and we are done,

01:03:35.438 --> 01:03:36.976
we have proven it to be false.

01:03:36.977 --> 01:03:37.997
So this works,

01:03:37.998 --> 01:03:41.617
and we have a proof that it
makes resolution complete.

01:03:43.424 --> 01:03:46.727
I mean, these three rules follow
a complete process for DQBF and

01:03:46.728 --> 01:03:49.244
we can solve in a very
symbolic way, I would say.

01:03:54.186 --> 01:03:57.705
Formulas that re-quantify our
functions, it's pretty cool.

01:03:57.706 --> 01:03:59.989
So far,
no implementation exists, so

01:03:59.990 --> 01:04:03.460
that's really hard to say
whether it's going somewhere.

01:04:03.461 --> 01:04:07.362
By hand, we were able to solve
some formulas by that that

01:04:07.363 --> 01:04:10.934
are sort of interesting,
but of course, that is so

01:04:10.935 --> 01:04:12.943
far just an exercise in 3D.

01:04:12.944 --> 01:04:15.977
I don't wanna claim I know how
to create an efficient solver

01:04:15.978 --> 01:04:16.714
around that.

01:04:16.715 --> 01:04:19.691
But there is a sort of
connection to [INAUDIBLE] that I

01:04:19.692 --> 01:04:22.540
believe is there and
I'm still working on that.

01:04:22.541 --> 01:04:25.644
Anyways, okay, now, okay,
I don't want to go into the last

01:04:25.645 --> 01:04:27.579
part because now
time is running out.

01:04:27.580 --> 01:04:30.863
It's, anyways,
a very interesting thing,

01:04:30.864 --> 01:04:33.221
the quantitive synthesis part,

01:04:33.222 --> 01:04:37.192
we can approximate synthesis
create with SAT solvers.

01:04:37.193 --> 01:04:42.592
It's a really cool thing,
but I'll leave that out,

01:04:42.593 --> 01:04:46.202
and we're going to
jump to the end.

01:04:46.203 --> 01:04:49.507
Okay, I have presented
the incremental determinization

01:04:49.508 --> 01:04:50.792
algorithm for 2QBF.

01:04:50.793 --> 01:04:54.529
I sort of sketched out how
this could be maybe lifted to

01:04:54.530 --> 01:04:58.767
quantifying over general
functions, Boolean functions,

01:04:58.768 --> 01:04:59.608
of course.

01:04:59.609 --> 01:05:03.984
And there's more work in this
area that I would also be happy

01:05:03.985 --> 01:05:06.794
to talk about if
you're interested.

01:05:06.795 --> 01:05:10.496
I'm here all day, and
happy to take questions and

01:05:10.497 --> 01:05:11.740
discuss later.

01:05:11.741 --> 01:05:15.350
&gt;&gt; Can you just say what
qualities this is solving?

01:05:15.351 --> 01:05:16.217
&gt;&gt; Okay.

01:05:16.218 --> 01:05:17.373
&gt;&gt; What's the problem?

01:05:17.374 --> 01:05:23.426
&gt;&gt; The very short summary is,

01:05:26.686 --> 01:05:31.030
&gt;&gt; Okay, we solved that problem.

01:05:31.031 --> 01:05:35.363
You're given some function from
exponential space of natural

01:05:35.364 --> 01:05:39.295
numbers to non-exponential
space of natural numbers.

01:05:39.296 --> 01:05:42.380
And where the function can be
sharply hard to compute for

01:05:42.381 --> 01:05:45.332
a single input, we have
exponentially many inputs,

01:05:45.333 --> 01:05:46.918
each input is sharply hard.

01:05:46.919 --> 01:05:49.305
And we have no
convexity assumption,

01:05:49.306 --> 01:05:53.183
no assumption at all about this
function, not even that it has

01:05:53.184 --> 01:05:55.806
an informative derivative or
anything.

01:05:55.807 --> 01:06:00.587
And [INAUDIBLE] you can
find an input that is

01:06:00.588 --> 01:06:04.280
probably close to the maximum.

01:06:04.281 --> 01:06:07.714
And that with only pulling
normally many class SAT solvers,

01:06:07.715 --> 01:06:09.000
that's the shorter.

01:06:09.001 --> 01:06:11.155
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE].

01:06:11.156 --> 01:06:13.244
&gt;&gt; It's through model counting,
and

01:06:13.245 --> 01:06:17.001
there's a very neat trick how we
can extend that to actually turn

01:06:17.002 --> 01:06:19.173
it into an optimization
algorithm.

01:06:22.669 --> 01:06:24.374
Yeah.

